1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,110
Listen to a talk in a sociology class.

2
00:00:05,110 --> 00:00:09,258
Social capital is the network of relationships among people who live

3
00:00:09,258 --> 00:00:11,090
and work in a particular society.

4
00:00:11,090 --> 00:00:14,220
These networks enable society to function effectively.

5
00:00:14,220 --> 00:00:20,082
Social capital includes things like trust, mutual understanding and shared values,

6
00:00:20,082 --> 00:00:25,040
and also behaviors that bind people together and facilitate cooperation.

7
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:28,936
Social capital can be divided into two main types:

8
00:00:28,936 --> 00:00:32,150
bonding social capital and bridging social capital.

9
00:00:32,150 --> 00:00:39,170
Bonding social capital refers to the tight bonds within a close-knit group, like family members or close friends.

10
00:00:39,170 --> 00:00:44,200
These strong ties are essential for providing support and assistance during times of need.

11
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:51,000
Bridging social capital, on the other hand, involves connections that transcend different social groups

12
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,150
like relationships between acquaintances or colleagues from different backgrounds.

13
00:00:55,150 --> 00:01:00,250
These weaker ties are crucial for accessing new information and opportunities.

14
00:01:00,250 --> 00:01:05,000
The benefits of social capital are numerous.

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:09,920
It can give people emotional support, increase their access to resources and information

16
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:12,210
and foster communal cooperation.

17
00:01:12,210 --> 00:01:19,230
And interestingly, people and communities with higher levels of social capital actually tend to have better health.

18
00:01:19,230 --> 00:01:23,310
Next, we will discuss how social capital can be measured

19
00:01:23,310 --> 00:01:27,290
and the challenges associated with its quantification.
